rockchip-vaapi (3.5.0~rc1-1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Release candidate 1 for 3.5.0: 4K60 AV1 in Firefox.
  * AV1: a picture the client syncs before its successor arrives (Firefox,
    FFmpeg's download path) is decoded once instead of twice when MPP has the
    deferred reference refresh (protocol 2, librockchip-mpp1 +pd32 or later,
    RK3588): MPP keeps the early decode and the driver commits its reference
    slots later ('H'/'C' padding OBUs with a token, enabled per decoder).
    Other MPP builds keep the re-decode (KI-14). RKVA_AV1_NO_DEFER=1 forces it.
  * AV1: EndPicture never waits: packets go to MPP from a per-decoder submit
    thread (at most 16 queued). Synthesised show_existing headers are closed
    with a temporal delimiter, so MPP emits them at once with their own PTS
    (fixes 3 s sync timeouts at temporal units with hidden pictures). The
    show_existing of a re-shown KEY names a slot that really holds it (MPP's
    reference slots are tracked).
  * All codecs: a per-decoder output thread routes MPP's frames as they come
    out and vaSyncSurface waits on the surface instead of polling every 1 ms
    (RKVA_SYNC_POLL=1 restores polling). Packets carry the surface generation
    in their PTS; an output for a surface the client already reused is
    dropped. vaSyncSurface returns at once when its decoder is destroyed.
  * 8-bit zero-copy: for clients that export each surface after decoding
    (Firefox, mpv), frames are exported from MPP's own buffer (64-aligned
    strides only) and held until the surface is rendered into again; clients
    that read the surface's own buffer (Chromium, vaDeriveImage/vaGetImage,
    writable exports) get the copy when they read. RKVA_ZERO_COPY=0 turns it
    off.
  * Teardown: Terminate stops every decoder's threads before freeing
    surfaces; DestroySurfaces excludes frame routing while it frees.
  * Log lines carry a monotonic timestamp and thread id, plus per-step times.
  * Recommends librockchip-mpp1 (>= ...+pd32~) for the single-decode path.
  * Measured on an Orange Pi 5B (Plaid Pi 5.0, RK3588S): Firefox 157 YouTube
    BBB 4K60 AV1 drops 91-95 % with 3.0.0 + stock MPP, about 8-9 % with
    this driver and MPP, 1.2-1.5 % with media.video-queue.hw-accel-size 10.

 -- defcom5-rockchip <286489597+defcom5-rockchip@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:28:15 +0000

rockchip-vaapi (3.0.0-1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Release 3.0.0 "Plaid Number Nine": AV1 hardware decode (Main profile,
    8- and 10-bit). VA-API clients deliver parsed parameters and tile data;
    MPP wants a complete OBU stream, so src/av1.c rebuilds the sequence and
    frame headers with a vendored copy of GStreamer's AV1 bit writer (five
    upstream bugs fixed in the copy, segmentation implemented). refresh_frame_flags, which VA-API does
    not pass, comes from the next picture's reference map: pictures are held
    until their successor arrives, decoded at once when the client syncs
    them. Hidden frames are shown through a synthesised show_existing
    (hidden KEY frames through a second decode as INTRA_ONLY), film grain is
    not applied (MPP #974), the picture lands on current_display_picture.
    Matches the Fluster reference checksums on 213/242 libaom and 31/36
    Chromium conformance vectors, the rest explained in KI-13; decode on
    the AV1 engine confirmed in ffmpeg, mpv, Chrome and Firefox.
  * RKVA_HIDE_10BIT now also makes every profile 8-bit only and refuses a
    10-bit AV1 picture (8- and 10-bit AV1 share one profile); RKVA_HIDE_AV1
    hides AV1; RKVA_AV1_WRITER_DEBUG and RKVA_DUMP_AV1 for diagnosis.
  * Pictures larger than the surface they were created for (a sequence
    change inside one context) are cropped instead of overrunning the
    buffer; vaGetImage is bounded by the image's own size.
  * Slice parameters are paired with the data buffer before them as well
    (Chromium submits tile data first).
  * Vendor string: Rockchip MPP VA-API Driver 3.0.0 "Plaid Number Nine"
    (defcom5). Named after Number Nine's Pepper co-processor boards.

